/* book Worked example 2: Q: A receiver sees signal power S over white noise such that S/N₀ = 1.5 × 10⁷ Hz. Find the capacity for B = 1 MHz, for B = 2 MHz, and for B → ∞. Then find the capacity at B = 1 MHz if instead the signal power is doubled.
   1. B = 1 MHz: N = N₀B, so S/N = 1.5 × 10⁷ / 10⁶ = 15. C = 10⁶ log₂ 16 = 4.00 Mbit/s.
   2. B = 2 MHz: S/N = 1.5 × 10⁷ / (2 × 10⁶) = 7.5. C = 2 × 10⁶ log₂ 8.5 = 2 × 10⁶ × 3.087 = 6.17 Mbit/s.
   3. B → ∞: C → 1.443 × 1.5 × 10⁷ = 21.6 Mbit/s, the ceiling no bandwidth can pass.
   4. Double the power at B = 1 MHz: S/N = 30, C = 10⁶ log₂ 31 = 10⁶ × 4.954 = 4.95 Mbit/s.
   5. Compare: doubling the bandwidth added 2.17 Mbit/s; doubling the power added 0.95 Mbit/s. At this S/N bandwidth is the stronger lever, but each further doubling of B adds less, because the band is already noise-heavy.
   Answer: C = 4.00 Mbit/s at 1 MHz, 6.17 Mbit/s at 2 MHz and at most 21.6 Mbit/s however wide the band; doubling the power at 1 MHz gives only 4.95 Mbit/s. */
