/* book exam traps: Treating the theorem as a construction. It proves that a code exists; it does not give the parity-check matrix, encoder or decoder. | Believing a better decoder can push Pe to zero when R > C. The converse forbids it for every decoder. | Calling R = k/n a bit rate in bit/s. R is information bits per channel use; the bit rate also needs the channel symbol rate. | Using p = 1% as p = 1 in H(p). Probabilities in entropy formulas must be between 0 and 1; 1% is 0.01. | Thinking a single parity check corrects one error. It detects an odd number of errors; it does not locate or correct them. */
