/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A binary linear block code takes k message bits and produces an n-bit codeword; it is called an (n,k) code.
   - Systematic form puts the message bits in plain view: G = [Ik | P].
   - w(c) is the Hamming weight, the number of 1 bits in c; dmin is the minimum Hamming distance between any two distinct codewords.
   - The Hamming distance d(c₁,c₂) is the number of positions where two words differ.
   - Send c through a noisy channel and receive r.
   - Minimum distance decides the guarantee.
   - The standard array is the same idea in one line: write all 2k codewords in the first row, then add the most likely error pattern (coset leader) to every codeword of that row, and repeat for every coset.
   - Hardware follows the algebra.
   - The Hamming (7,4) code has n = 7, k = 4 and n − k = 3 parity bits.
   - The Hamming bound for t = 1 is 2n−k ≥ 1 + n.
*/
