/* book exam traps: Reading the syndrome as a decimal error position. In the (7,4) example 111 is a 3-bit column label; it points to column 4 because of the chosen H, not to bit 7. | Forgetting that dmin = 3 detects two errors but corrects only one. A two-bit error can land on a syndrome that the table maps to a single-bit pattern, so the decoder may miscorrect. | Mixing the blocks in G and H. Systematic G is [Ik | P], not [P | Ik]; H is [PT | In−k]. Swapping them breaks GHT = 0. | Using a different column order in H than the one used to build the syndrome table. The syndrome is only a pointer to that table. */
